# Book pages contain stories; mechanical help belongs in the reading UI and abilities menu.

book-story-decipherer-title = Seven Unsigned Letters
book-story-decipherer-text =
    Seven letters from a supposedly dead merchant all asked for candles. A scribe noticed the same misspelling moving one word further in each letter. Reading along its path, he found an account of prisoners held in an old saltworks. A week later the rescued merchant handed him an eighth letter. His jailer had taken his tongue, but not his words.

book-story-dice-title = The Last Game at Three Geese
book-story-dice-text =
    Mert had three coppers and a chipped die left. While the innkeeper argued with the cook, he turned the die beneath his thumb as his uncle had taught him. Six dots landed face up. Mert reclaimed his boots, but the cook stopped him at the door. She held the die with its chipped corner against her nail. She had known his uncle too.

book-story-lips-title = A Conversation Behind Glass
book-story-lips-text =
    After her illness Liora could no longer hear the bell, but she watched the pigeons rise from its tower. Through a merchant's window she saw her brother laughing while his lips named a place. By comparing that movement with conversations at the well, she found him before dawn. When he asked who had warned her, she asked him to repeat the question without turning away.

book-story-porter-title = A Chest on the Pass
book-story-porter-text =
    Four merchants had failed to carry a chest over a broken bridge. The porter removed its projecting brackets and bound boards and straps around it. It became an awkward pack, no lighter than before. At the far end of the pass he restored the fittings and charged twice: once for the load, once for listening to four merchants' advice.

book-story-traps-title = Rug's Trap
book-story-traps-text =
    Rug found a child's shoe beside his trap and a stick between its jaws. The girl he found nearby had pinned the spring with a split log and held the jaw with a strap before freeing an animal's paw. He looked at her unhurt fingers and said nothing. That spring they spent an evening taking a broken lock apart. A stick hung over his door ever after.

book-story-mixtures-title = The Widow's Two Vials
book-story-mixtures-text =
    Lamp oil had spoiled the healer's last tincture. His widow brought a second vessel, warmed the mixture between her hands and waited for the cloudy bands to settle. By dawn one vial held dark oil and the other a clear infusion. A neighbour credited her husband's ghost. She showed him her stained sleeves: he had spent twenty years teaching her patience.

book-story-workshop-title = The Workshop That Walked Away
book-story-workshop-text =
    The river took Brom's walls. His sons rescued the money and their grandfather's portrait. Brom knocked out the bench's wedges, folded its legs along the top and lashed the vice in place. The bundle was heavy enough to require six rests. By morning he was working at the reassembled bench. Now, he said, they could build some walls.

book-story-bonds-title = The Ferryman's Rope
book-story-bonds-text =
    Bandits bound a ferryman with his own rope. While they laughed at his concern for his boots, he drew a breath and eased his wrists apart. Once the rope was wet he exhaled, lowered a shoulder and found its short end. A month later he moored their boat so thoroughly that three men could not untie it before the watch arrived.

book-story-maps-title = The Tower That Wasn't There
book-story-maps-text =
    Her father's map placed a tower by the ford, but Eda found its stones under water. She marked the river bend and three pines instead, counted her steps to the village and checked the road against sunset. A year later her father escaped the same mistake by following a stranger's map. He recognised his daughter's handwriting beside a well.

book-story-taming-title = The Lost Wolf Pup

book-story-transplant-title = Reaching for the Sun

book-story-traces-title = Ash on Blue Snow
book-story-traces-text =
    The watch blamed lightning for the burnt gate. An investigator rubbed ash onto white cloth and recognised the broken pattern of a fireball spell. The residue clung to iron as elven magic did. The elf they found admitted casting it, then asked them to inspect the gate's inner face. There were desperate axe marks on that side.

book-story-voices-title = The Witness in the Empty Barrel
book-story-voices-text =
    At the fair a cooper was accused of theft. An empty barrel beside the judge demanded that the true thief be summoned. Beneath the platform a boy barely moved his lips, watching its wooden hoop as he spoke. The accuser fled first. The grateful cooper later made the boy a little barrel, which he sold as a home for a very opinionated spirit.

book-story-riposte-title = The Master's Empty Hand
book-story-riposte-text =
    Each day the apprentice struck harder; each day his sword ended on the floor. The master showed him the spot near the guard where the answering pressure twisted his wrist open. The following morning the master's sword fell for the first time. The apprentice grinned, missing the wooden knife in the old man's other hand.

book-story-guard-title = A Crack in the Oak Shield
book-story-guard-text =
    Gart's shield had survived three sieges. A thin soldier struck its edges where the straps carried the force into Gart's elbow, changing height until his arm swung aside. Afterwards Gart brought him bread in the infirmary and asked to see the movement slowly. Inside the repaired shield he wrote: hands hold it, not oak.

book-story-arrows-title = Rain Over the Training Yard
book-story-arrows-text =
    The captain began with reeds tipped in cloth. His pupils learned to watch the archer's shoulder and meet each reed with a short movement of the blade's flat. They called it a child's game until the siege. One pupil deflected an arrow before he had time to be afraid, then kept it beside his first broken reed.

book-story-march-title = The Standard-Bearer's Last Steps
book-story-march-text =
    Six soldiers held a passage while the wounded reached the boats. The standard-bearer took a fallen comrade's shield and retreated in small sideways steps, finding each stone before shifting his weight. He could not stop every blow, but he never blocked the others' retreat. His flag reached the boats first and dried above the rescued men.

book-story-shot-title = Two Shadows in the Gate
book-story-shot-text =
    The huntress chose her straightest shaft while two enemies lined up in the gateway. She settled her elbow and drew to the old scar on her cheek. One arrow struck both shadows. Afterwards the smith asked how she had enchanted its head. She showed him her strained hands; she could not have repeated that shot immediately.

book-story-grip-title = A Duel Outside the Laundry
book-story-grip-text =
    A porter lifted the tailor by his collar. Instead of pulling at the hand, the tailor stepped closer, dropped his shoulder and twisted the cloth away from the gripping fingers. He escaped with a torn flap. When the porter later brought his own coat for repairs, the tailor added a stout inner loop. This time the porter had something to learn.

book-story-scribing-title = Ink After Midnight
book-story-scribing-text =
    The scribe was dying and the last page remained blank. His pupil's copied flourishes produced only blots. Told to put the sample away, the pupil recalled the heat and sequence of a spell he knew himself. By midnight the page was warm. In the morning it kindled a little flame, enough to light his teacher's funeral candle.

book-story-common-title = The Price of One Word
book-story-common-text =
    An elven envoy kept asking to buy a riverbank instead of a crossing. The ferryman drew a boat in the sand, and they spent the evening giving each object two names. A year later the envoy returned without an interpreter and asked after his host's wife. He had also learned to argue about the fare.

book-story-elf-title = A Guest Beneath the Rowan
book-story-elf-text =
    A traveller greeted his elven hostess with the name of a bitter berry. She served him a bowlful, then spent the afternoon naming branches, birds and their shadows, changing each phrase for past and future. He wrote whole sentences in his journal. He never crossed out his first greeting, but drew some berries beside it.

book-story-orc-title = Supper at the Northern Gate
book-story-orc-text =
    The cook mistook an orc caravan's loud demand for a challenge. They wanted salt. Over supper the caravan master taught him phrases while the drovers corrected his stress. A small change could turn a guest into someone under one's protection. By morning the cook knew how to address his guests, though the inn still had no salt.

book-story-goblin-title = The Bill for a Leaking Pot
book-story-goblin-text =
    A goblin charged separately for a hole and the broken bottom around it. The owner sat beside him and made him explain their agreement word by word. Tiny endings distinguished a promise from a possibility and a possession from a find. A week later she bargained without an interpreter and charged him meals for the lessons.

book-story-tribe-title = Names for Rain
book-story-tribe-text =
    A guide stopped the party under a sunny sky and answered a question about rain with four different words. That night the flood erased their trail. Around the fire she explained rain in the distance, rain overhead, water in the earth and water still seeking a course. The traveller filled his notebook with her stories, and refused to sell it when he returned.

book-story-ancient-title = A Will Without Heirs
book-story-ancient-text =
    Rulers had built increasingly absurd detours around a stone believed to bear a curse. An archivist recognised one sign on a measuring cup and another in a mill's inventory. By winter he could read the inscription: the owner had left his well for everyone to use. The road was straightened, and a translation hung beneath the stone.

book-story-cursed-title = A Letter Behind a Prayer
book-story-cursed-text =
    Behind a prayer for sleep were lines its owner had forbidden anyone to speak aloud. A translator began with recurring names: one was an inherited debt, another changed when an oath was made to the dead. Beneath the threatening shapes she found a plea to return a name surrendered for a child's life. Both sides bore the same signature.

book-story-growling-title = A Night at the Beasts' Fire
book-story-growling-text =
    The captive merchant thought the growling figures were deciding who would eat him. Then they handed him a bowl. Along the road he learned to distinguish names, requests and jokes, noting how tongue and lips changed the sounds. Released at the border with his ransom untouched, he began his account with an apology to his former companions.

book-story-hissing-title = The Shop Behind the Silk Curtain
book-story-hissing-text =
    A customer mistook a trader's hissing question for dismissal. He stayed to help until the rains ended, learning how short whistles formed words and how a long breath changed meaning with its place in a phrase. By departure he could tell a story without gestures. She gave him a silk ribbon, the first word he had learned.
